 82	// APPLYING a status transition — the write half of the vocabulary whose read half
 83	// is `work-item.mjs`'s decode (docs/PRINCIPLES.md, the migration of #1119).
 84	//
 85	// One rule, and it is why this is not a plain `swapLabel`: the item may wear ANY
 86	// engine version's spelling of the status it is leaving. A member's items outlive
 87	// its converges, so an item filed as `task:ready` is picked up by an engine that
 88	// writes `task:status:waiting-for-executor`, and a swap that named one spelling
 89	// would leave the other standing — an item wearing two live statuses, which is the
 90	// torn state the repair phase exists to repair.
 91	//
 92	// Writes stay granular (add and remove named labels, never a set-write): with
 93	// several executors and a scheduler run moving labels at once, a set-write from a
 94	// stale snapshot clobbers a concurrent transition (PRINCIPLES.md).
 95	// The removes are UNCONDITIONAL — every spelling of the status being left, not
 96	// only the ones the caller's snapshot shows. A caller holds the labels it read
