  1	// The executor's entry gate: identify the ONE dispatch this session was started
  2	// for, and validate it in code BEFORE any model judgment (per-project-scheduling
  3	// PRINCIPLES.md). This is the CLI shell `validate-dispatch.mjs` was written to be
  4	// driven by — it wires that pure core's `exists` / `isPackDeclared` / `loadTask`
  5	// capabilities to this checkout and hands it the issue body.
  6	//
  7	// IT DOES NOT CLAIM. The claim protocol (read labels → swap ready → agent-running
  8	// → post a claim comment → re-read, earliest claim wins) needs GitHub WRITES,
  9	// which the executor session can only make through its MCP tools, so it stays
 10	// agent-driven prose in public/instructions.md. This shell only decides "is there a dispatch
 11	// here, is it mine, and is it legal".
 12	//
 13	// TWO TRIGGER SOURCES, because the same `issues.labeled` webhook reaches an
 14	// executor session by two different transports and only one of them was ever
 15	// read here:
 16	//
 17	//   1. GITHUB ACTIONS writes the whole webhook payload to disk at
 18	//      `$GITHUB_EVENT_PATH` — `action`, `label.name` and the entire `issue`
 19	//      object, `issue.number` and `issue.body` included. Payload plus local
 20	//      checkout is everything the validation needs, in one shot.
 21	//   2. CLAUDE CODE ON THE WEB (CCR) delivers the same trigger as environment
 22	//      variables instead — `CCR_TRIGGER_SOURCE`, `CCR_TRIGGER_EVENT`,
 23	//      `CCR_TRIGGER_REPO`, `CCR_TRIGGER_ISSUE_NUMBER` — and writes no payload
 24	//      file at all. It NAMES the issue but carries neither the label that was
 25	//      added nor the body, so it resolves in two shots: this shell reports
 26	//      `needs-issue` with the number, the executor fetches that one issue over
 27	//      MCP, saves the tool's raw JSON response verbatim to a file, and
 28	//      re-invokes with `--issue-json` for the identical validation. The shell
 29	//      extracts body, labels, and title from the response itself — the agent
 30	//      pastes bytes, it never hand-extracts fields — and rejects a response
 31	//      whose `number` is not the trigger's, so fetching the wrong issue is
 32	//      caught in code. (`--issue-body-file` + `--issue-labels` remain as the
 33	//      manual fallback should the fetch's response shape ever surprise.)
 34	//
 35	// Reading only source 1 is what made every CCR-run executor session miss its own
 36	// trigger and select an issue by listing instead — the duplicate-execution bug
 37	// re-entered through the front door. Observed live 2026-07-28: two sessions each
 38	// selected dispatch #772 and claimed it one second apart.
 39	//
 40	// ZERO NETWORK, BY CONSTRUCTION — still. The executor session is MCP-only and
 41	// carries no repo credential of its own, so anything reaching the GitHub REST API
 42	// here would both fail to authenticate and trip the in-session-github-access
 43	// rule. The CCR path does not change that: this shell never fetches the issue
 44	// itself, it tells the EXECUTOR to fetch it over MCP and hand the bytes back.
 45	//
 46	// THE VERDICT IS THE INTERFACE; THE EXIT CODE IS ONLY "did the routine break".
 47	// The executor branches on the printed `dispatch:` field, which every decided
 48	// verdict carries. The exit code answers a narrower question, and answers it the
 49	// way every other command does (owner, 2026-08-14): ZERO WHEN THE ROUTINE GOES
 50	// ON — including when going on means stopping on purpose — and NON-ZERO ONLY
 51	// WHEN IT STOPS UNEXPECTEDLY. A verdict this shell was written to reach is not a
 52	// failure, and a harness that paints exit 11 red teaches both the reader and the
 53	// agent to read an ordinary "not mine" as a fault.
 54	//
 55	// Exit 0, `dispatch:` names which one:
 56	//
 57	//   valid       — a legal dispatch for this session's scope; go claim it.
 58	//   needs-issue — a CCR trigger named the issue but carries no body/labels.
 59	//                 Fetch THAT issue over MCP and re-invoke with them. The routine
 60	//                 is mid-handshake, not stopped.
 61	//   invalid     — a forged or mangled dispatch. Comment the printed `reason`,
 62	//                 remove the ready label, add `needs-human` + `task:needs-human-failure`,
 63	//                 end the session.
 64	//                 It never runs. Prescribed work, so: zero.
 65	//   task-gone   — a well-formed dispatch naming a task this repo no longer
 66	//                 carries (file removed, pack undeclared). CLOSE the issue
 67	//                 with the printed reason (owner, 2026-08-06) — an obsolete
 68	//                 dispatch is not a human's problem. It never runs.
 69	//   not-mine    — the trigger label is no ready label at all, or the issue no
 70	//                 longer carries one (a dispatch another session has already
 71	//                 claimed). Stop. Change nothing, comment nothing. An expected
 72	//                 stop is not an error.
 73	//
 74	// Non-zero — the routine stops and something needs a human:
 75	//
 76	//   12 no-trigger      — NO source names an issue. STOP THE SESSION. There is no
 77	//                        fallback: never select a dispatch by listing (see below).
 78	//   15 scope-mismatch  — the trigger label is the OTHER executor's ready label.
 79	//                        Each routine fires on its own label, so this is a
 80	//                        MISCONFIGURED ROUTINE (a fleet routine whose prompt lost
 81	//                        the word `fleet`, most often), not a dispatch to adopt.
 82	//                        Stop — change nothing, comment nothing — but stop LOUDLY:
 83	//                        nothing on GitHub records this, and the next run files the
 84	//                        dispatch, and it declines forever until a human reads it.
 85	//   2  usage           — bad invocation (an unknown scope argument, an unreadable
 86	//                        `--issue-body-file`).
 87	//   1  internal        — an unexpected fault in this shell.
 88	//
 89	// Codes 10, 11, 13 and 14 are RETIRED, not renumbered — every one of them now
 90	// exits 0 under its own `dispatch:` name, and no live code carries a changed
 91	// meaning for a reader who remembers the old table.
 92	//
 93	// THERE IS NO FALLBACK, BY DESIGN. Exit 12 used to send the executor off to list
 94	// the open dispatches and take the oldest. That is precisely the
 95	// N-sessions-racing-over-N-issues failure the one-session-one-issue rule exists to
 96	// prevent, reached from the other direction: one scheduler run files every due
 97	// dispatch seconds apart, so every session that cannot name its own trigger builds
 98	// the SAME work list and races over it. A session that does not know its issue
 99	// must run nothing - the next scheduler run files the occurrence again if the work
100	// is still owed, so stopping costs a delay while guessing costs duplicated work.
101	//
102	// Usage: `node <engine>/scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs [self|fleet]`
103	//                `[--issue-json <path> | --issue-body-file <path> --issue-labels <csv>]`
104	// The positional argument is THIS SESSION's scope — which of the two executor
105	// routines is running. It defaults to `self`, which is every ordinary project's
106	// executor; the FLEET routine must pass `fleet` explicitly, and a fleet payload
107	// arriving at a session that did not is reported as not-mine with that stated
108	// plainly. The flags carry what a CCR trigger cannot, and are ignored on the
109	// Actions path (whose payload already has everything).
110	
111	import { existsSync, readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
112	import { dirname, join, sep } from 'node:path';
113	import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';
114	import { DISPATCH_PATH_RE, dispatchFirstLine, validateDispatchBody } from './validate-dispatch.mjs';
115	import { parseDispatchTitle, readyLabelForScope, EXECUTOR_SCOPES } from './dispatch.mjs';
116	import { renderTaskExec } from '../items/run-record.mjs';
117	import { opensPullRequest } from '../contract/task-contract.mjs';
118	import { findTaskDeclaration, loadTaskDeclaration } from '../contract/task-declaration.mjs';
119	import { policyExpression } from '../contract/merge-policy.mjs';
120	import { SHARED_SUBDIR } from '../../../../engine/pack_loader/pack-registry.mjs';
121	import { actionsEnv } from '../world/actions.mjs';
122	
123	export const EXIT = {
124	  ok: 0,
125	  internal: 1,
126	  usage: 2,
127	  noTrigger: 12,
128	  scopeMismatch: 15,
129	};
130	
131	// The executor scope a ready label implies — the exact inverse of the mapping the
132	// SCHEDULER files a dispatch under (`readyLabelForScope`), derived from it rather
133	// than restated, so the two can never drift. `null` = not a ready label at all.
134	export const scopeForLabel = (label) =>
135	  EXECUTOR_SCOPES.find((scope) => readyLabelForScope(scope) === label) ?? null;
136	
137	// Which checkout do the task paths in a dispatch body resolve against? Answered
138	// from where THIS engine copy is mounted, not from cwd — a consumer runs the
139	// vendored engine at `<root>/.claudinite/shared/packs/claudinite-tasks/`, the canon
140	// repo runs its own at `<root>/packs/claudinite-tasks/`. Deriving it from the module's
141	// own location means whichever copy the executor invoked resolves against that
142	// copy's own repo, with nothing to pass — and nothing for the instructions to explain.
143	const MOUNT_SUFFIX = sep + SHARED_SUBDIR;
144	export function repoRootFrom(moduleUrl) {
145	  // <home>/packs/claudinite-tasks/src/session/<this file>
146	  const home = dirname(dirname(dirname(dirname(dirname(fileURLToPath(moduleUrl))))));
147	  return home.endsWith(MOUNT_SUFFIX) ? home.slice(0, -MOUNT_SUFFIX.length) : home;
148	}
149	
150	// Read the webhook payload the label event was started with. Every way of not
151	// having one collapses to a single `{ error }` — unset, unreadable, unparsable —
152	// because the caller's response to all of them is the same: try the other
153	// trigger source, and stop the session if that names no issue either.
154	export function readEventPayload(env = actionsEnv(), read = (p) => readFileSync(p, 'utf8')) {
155	  const path = env.GITHUB_EVENT_PATH;
156	  if (!path) return { error: 'GITHUB_EVENT_PATH is not set — this session has no event payload on disk' };
157	  let raw;
158	  try {
159	    raw = read(path);
160	  } catch (e) {
161	    return { error: `GITHUB_EVENT_PATH points at ${path}, which is unreadable: ${e.message}` };
162	  }
163	  let event;
164	  try {
165	    event = JSON.parse(raw);
166	  } catch (e) {
167	    return { error: `the event payload at ${path} is not valid JSON: ${e.message}` };
168	  }
169	  if (event === null || typeof event !== 'object' || Array.isArray(event)) {
170	    return { error: `the event payload at ${path} is not an event object` };
171	  }
172	  return { event };
173	}
174	
175	// The three facts a dispatch trigger must carry. Anything short of all three
176	// means the payload cannot name this session's issue — not a rejection of a
177	// dispatch, just this source failing to identify one; the caller tries the other
178	// source before giving up.
179	export function triggerFromEvent(event) {
180	  const action = event.action;
181	  const label = event.label?.name;
182	  const number = event.issue?.number;
183	  if (action !== 'labeled') return { error: `the event payload is a "${action}" event, not a label event — it names no dispatch` };
184	  if (typeof label !== 'string' || label === '') return { error: 'the label event carries no label.name' };
185	  if (!Number.isInteger(number)) return { error: 'the label event carries no issue.number' };
186	  return { trigger: { source: 'payload', label, number, body: event.issue?.body ?? '', title: event.issue?.title ?? '' } };
187	}
188	
189	// The CCR trigger: same webhook, delivered as environment variables by Claude
190	// Code on the web, which writes no payload file. It names the issue and nothing
191	// else — no `label.name`, no `issue.body` — so the trigger it yields is
192	// deliberately PARTIAL, and `main` turns that into the two-shot `needs-issue`
193	// handshake rather than guessing either missing field.
194	export function triggerFromCcrEnv(env = actionsEnv()) {
195	  const source = env.CCR_TRIGGER_SOURCE;
196	  const event = env.CCR_TRIGGER_EVENT;
197	  const raw = env.CCR_TRIGGER_ISSUE_NUMBER;
198	  if (!source && !event && !raw) return { error: 'no CCR_TRIGGER_* variables are set either — this session carries no CCR trigger' };
199	  if (source !== 'github') return { error: `CCR_TRIGGER_SOURCE is ${JSON.stringify(source ?? null)}, not "github" — this session was not started by a GitHub event` };
200	  if (event !== 'issues.labeled') return { error: `CCR_TRIGGER_EVENT is ${JSON.stringify(event ?? null)}, not "issues.labeled" — it names no dispatch` };
201	  const number = Number(raw);
202	  if (!raw || !Number.isInteger(number) || number <= 0) return { error: `CCR_TRIGGER_ISSUE_NUMBER is ${JSON.stringify(raw ?? null)}, which is not an issue number` };
203	  return { trigger: { source: 'ccr', number, repo: env.CCR_TRIGGER_REPO ?? '' } };
204	}
205	
206	// Identify this session's ONE dispatch from whichever transport carried it.
207	// Order is preference, not precedence-by-truth: the Actions payload is tried
208	// first only because it answers in one shot, and a session that somehow has both
209	// gets the same issue from either. Every failure of both sources is collected
210	// into one reason, because the executor prints it and then stops.
211	export function resolveTrigger(env = actionsEnv(), read) {
212	  const reasons = [];
213	  const { event, error: payloadError } = readEventPayload(env, read);
214	  if (payloadError) reasons.push(payloadError);
215	  else {
216	    const { trigger, error } = triggerFromEvent(event);
217	    if (trigger) return { trigger };
218	    reasons.push(error);
219	  }
220	  const { trigger: ccr, error: ccrError } = triggerFromCcrEnv(env);
221	  if (ccr) return { trigger: ccr };
222	  reasons.push(ccrError);
223	  return { error: reasons.join('; ') };
224	}
225	
226	// `[scope] [--flag value|--flag=value]`. Kept deliberately tiny — the shell takes
227	// one positional and two flags, and a dependency-free parser is less surface than
228	// the alternative.
229	export function parseArgs(argv) {
230	  const positional = [];
231	  const flags = {};
232	  for (let i = 0; i < argv.length; i++) {
233	    const arg = argv[i];
234	    if (!arg.startsWith('--')) { positional.push(arg); continue; }
235	    const eq = arg.indexOf('=');
236	    if (eq !== -1) flags[arg.slice(2, eq)] = arg.slice(eq + 1);
237	    else flags[arg.slice(2)] = argv[++i] ?? '';
238	  }
239	  return { positional, flags };
240	}
241	
242	// Which of the issue's CURRENT labels is a ready label? On the CCR path this
243	// stands in for the `label.name` the trigger never carried — and it is strictly
244	// more informative than the event's: a dispatch another session already claimed
245	// no longer carries a ready label at all, so this catches the lost race here at
246	// step 1 rather than at the claim.
247	export function readyLabelAmong(labels) {
248	  const ready = labels.filter((l) => scopeForLabel(l) !== null);
249	  if (ready.length === 0) return { error: `it carries no ready label (it has: ${labels.join(', ') || 'none'}) — its dispatch has already been claimed or converged by another session` };
250	  if (ready.length > 1) return { error: `it carries more than one ready label (${ready.join(', ')}), so which executor owns it is ambiguous` };
251	  return { label: ready[0] };
252	}
253	
254	// The CCR handshake's payload: the raw issue-fetch response, saved verbatim.
255	// The whole point is that the executor pastes bytes instead of hand-extracting
256	// fields — a body threaded through the shell mangles quoting, a hand-assembled
257	// labels CSV drops one. Accepts both label shapes (the MCP tools return plain
258	// strings, the REST API returns { name } objects), and surfaces `number` so the
259	// caller can assert the agent fetched the issue the trigger actually names.
260	export function parseIssueJson(raw) {
261	  let issue;
262	  try {
263	    issue = JSON.parse(raw);
264	  } catch (e) {
265	    return { error: `not valid JSON: ${e.message}` };
266	  }
267	  if (issue === null || typeof issue !== 'object' || Array.isArray(issue)) {
268	    return { error: 'not an issue object' };
269	  }
270	  const labels = (Array.isArray(issue.labels) ? issue.labels : [])
271	    .map((l) => (typeof l === 'string' ? l : l?.name))
272	    .filter((l) => typeof l === 'string' && l !== '');
273	  return { issue: {
274	    number: Number.isInteger(issue.number) ? issue.number : null,
275	    body: typeof issue.body === 'string' ? issue.body : '',
276	    labels,
277	    title: typeof issue.title === 'string' ? issue.title : '',
278	  } };
279	}
280	
281	// The block the executor reads. `key: value` lines, one fact per line: an agent
282	// quoting a field back must not have to parse prose, and a reader diffing two
283	// runs must see exactly what changed.
284	const block = (fields) => Object.entries(fields).map(([k, v]) => `${k}: ${v}`).join('\n');
285	
286	// A decided verdict, as a value: the `code` the process must exit with, the
287	// `fields` block to print on stdout (absent for a usage error, which prints no
288	// block at all), and the `advice` line for stderr. The decision is returned
289	// rather than printed so a caller can judge it without a process: printing and
290	// exiting belong to the shell wrapper at the foot of this file, and are the only
291	// things it does.
292	const done = (code, fields, advice) => ({ code, fields, advice });
293	const usage = (advice) => ({ code: EXIT.usage, advice });
294	
295	export async function resolveDispatch(argv = process.argv.slice(2), env = actionsEnv()) {
296	  const { positional, flags } = parseArgs(argv);
297	  const scopeGiven = positional.length > 0;
298	  const scope = positional[0] ?? 'self';
299	  if (!EXECUTOR_SCOPES.includes(scope)) {
300	    return usage(`unknown scope "${scope}" - usage: node resolve-dispatch.mjs [${EXECUTOR_SCOPES.join('|')}] [--issue-json <path> | --issue-body-file <path> --issue-labels <csv>]`);
301	  }
302	
303	  const { trigger, error: triggerError } = resolveTrigger(env);
304	  if (triggerError) {
305	    return done(EXIT.noTrigger, { dispatch: 'no-trigger', scope, reason: triggerError },
306	      `${triggerError}. No trigger source names an issue, so this session cannot know which dispatch it was started for. STOP: run nothing, change nothing, comment nothing, end the session. There is NO fallback - never pick an issue by listing ${readyLabelForScope(scope)}; every dispatch in that list already has its own session, and the next scheduler run files the occurrence again if the work is still owed.`);
307	  }
308	
309	  let { label, number, body } = trigger;
310	  let title = trigger.title ?? '';
311	
312	  // The CCR handshake: the trigger named the issue, the executor fetched it over
313	  // MCP, and hands back here — as the raw response, or the manual two-flag
314	  // fallback — the fields the environment could not carry.
315	  if (trigger.source === 'ccr') {
316	    const jsonFile = flags['issue-json'];
317	    const bodyFile = flags['issue-body-file'];
318	    const labelsCsv = flags['issue-labels'];
319	    let labels;
320	    if (jsonFile !== undefined) {
321	      let raw;
322	      try {
323	        raw = readFileSync(jsonFile, 'utf8');
324	      } catch (e) {
325	        return usage(`--issue-json ${jsonFile} is unreadable: ${e.message}`);
326	      }
327	      const { issue, error } = parseIssueJson(raw);
328	      if (error) {
329	        return usage(`--issue-json ${jsonFile} is ${error}`);
330	      }
331	      if (issue.number !== null && issue.number !== number) {
332	        return usage(`--issue-json carries issue #${issue.number}, but this session's trigger names #${number} — that is the wrong issue. Fetch #${number} alone and re-run.`);
333	      }
334	      ({ body, title } = issue);
335	      labels = issue.labels;
336	    } else if (bodyFile !== undefined && labelsCsv !== undefined) {
337	      try {
338	        body = readFileSync(bodyFile, 'utf8');
339	      } catch (e) {
340	        return usage(`--issue-body-file ${bodyFile} is unreadable: ${e.message}`);
341	      }
342	      labels = labelsCsv.split(',').map((l) => l.trim()).filter(Boolean);
343	    } else {
344	      return done(EXIT.ok, { dispatch: 'needs-issue', issue: number, scope, source: 'ccr', repo: trigger.repo || '(unset)' },
345	        `the CCR trigger names issue #${number} but carries neither its body nor its labels. Fetch ISSUE #${number} ALONE over MCP (the issue-get tool), save the tool's raw JSON response verbatim to a file, then re-run: node <engine>/scheduler/resolve-dispatch.mjs ${scope} --issue-json <path>. Do not list, select, or touch any other issue.`);
346	    }
347	    const { label: ready, error: labelError } = readyLabelAmong(labels);
348	    if (labelError) {
349	      return done(EXIT.ok, { dispatch: 'not-mine', issue: number, scope, labels: labels.join('|') || '(none)' },
350	        `issue #${number}: ${labelError}. Stop: change nothing, comment nothing.`);
351	    }
352	    label = ready;
353	  }
354	
355	  const labelScope = scopeForLabel(label);
356	  if (labelScope === null) {
357	    return done(EXIT.ok, { dispatch: 'not-mine', issue: number, scope, label },
358	      `issue #${number} was labeled "${label}", which is not a ready label — this is not an executor dispatch. Stop: change nothing, comment nothing.`);
359	  }
360	  // The one stop that is NOT ordinary. Each executor routine fires on its own
361	  // ready label, so a dispatch arriving under the other scope's label did not
362	  // wander in — the routine that woke for it is misconfigured, and nothing on
363	  // GitHub will ever say so. Hence non-zero: the session is the only place a
364	  // human can read it, and a green exit there reads as "nothing to see".
365	  if (labelScope !== scope) {
366	    return done(EXIT.scopeMismatch, { dispatch: 'scope-mismatch', issue: number, scope, label, labelScope },
367	      `issue #${number} is labeled "${label}", a ${labelScope}-scoped dispatch, but this session's scope is "${scope}"${scopeGiven ? '' : ' (the default — pass "fleet" if this IS the fleet executor)'}. Each routine fires on its own ready label, so this session's routine is misconfigured — it will decline every ${labelScope} dispatch until a human fixes its launcher prompt. Stop: change nothing, comment nothing, and say so plainly in your final message.`);
368	  }
369	
370	  // The checkout the dispatch's task path must resolve in. `exists` reads the
371	  // working tree; in an executor session that IS HEAD (a fresh checkout, nothing
372	  // written yet), which is what validate-dispatch means by "exists at HEAD".
373	  const root = env.CLAUDINITE_REPO_ROOT || repoRootFrom(import.meta.url);
374	  const { loadConfig } = await import('../../../../engine/checks/helpers/repo-context.mjs');
375	  const declared = new Set(loadConfig(root).packs);
376	
377	  // `loadTask` is SYNCHRONOUS by design — it keeps validate-dispatch's core pure
378	  // and sync-testable — but importing an .mjs is not, so the shell prefetches the
379	  // module here and the capability just replays the result (or rethrows the parse
380	  // failure, which is exactly what the core wants to report).
381	  const firstLine = dispatchFirstLine(body);
382	  const taskDir = join(root, dirname(firstLine));
383	  let loaded = null;
384	  let loadError = null;
385	  let terms = new Map();
386	  let termsError = null;
387	  if (DISPATCH_PATH_RE.test(firstLine) && existsSync(taskDir)) {
388	    try {
389	      const file = findTaskDeclaration(taskDir);
390	      if (file) loaded = await loadTaskDeclaration(file);
391	    } catch (e) {
392	      loadError = e;
393	    }
394	    try {
395	      const { loadTaskTerms } = await import('../contract/task-terms.mjs');
396	      terms = await loadTaskTerms(taskDir);
397	    } catch (e) {
398	      termsError = e;
399	    }
400	  }
401	
402	  const verdict = validateDispatchBody(body, {
403	    exists: (p) => existsSync(join(root, p)),
404	    isPackDeclared: (id) => declared.has(id),
405	    loadTask: () => { if (loadError) throw loadError; return loaded; },
406	    loadTerms: () => { if (termsError) throw termsError; return terms; },
407	  });
408	
409	  const slotForRecord = parseDispatchTitle(title)?.slotId ?? null;
410	  if (!verdict.ok) {
411	    // The execution record for a code-decided terminal verdict, printed HERE so
412	    // the count never depends on the agent remembering to run record-exec.
413	    const record = verdict.pack
414	      ? renderTaskExec({ pack: verdict.pack, task: verdict.task, slotId: slotForRecord, status: verdict.gone ? 'task-gone' : 'invalid' })
415	      : null;
416	    if (verdict.gone) {
417	      return done(EXIT.ok, {
418	        dispatch: 'task-gone', issue: number, scope, label, reason: verdict.reason,
419	        ...(record ? { record } : {}),
420	      },
421	        `issue #${number} names a task this repo no longer carries: ${verdict.reason}. It must not run and needs no human: comment the reason, CLOSE the issue (not planned), and end the session. Do not add "needs-human".`);
422	    }
423	    return done(EXIT.ok, {
424	      dispatch: 'invalid', issue: number, scope, label, reason: verdict.reason,
425	      ...(record ? { record } : {}),
426	    },
427	      `issue #${number} is not a valid dispatch: ${verdict.reason}. It must not run — comment naming what failed, remove the "${label}" label, add "needs-human" and "task:needs-human-failure", and end the session.`);
428	  }
429	
430	  // `brief` is the one line the executor quotes prominently in chat before it
431	  // acts, so a human skimming the session sees at a glance which task ran and
432	  // under what parameters. The slot comes from the dispatch title when the
433	  // trigger carried one (the Actions payload and --issue-json both do; the
434	  // manual two-flag fallback does not).
435	  const slot = slotForRecord;
436	  return done(EXIT.ok, {
437	    dispatch: 'valid',
438	    brief: `Task: ${verdict.pack}/${verdict.task}${slot ? ` (slot ${slot})` : ''} — issue #${number}, model ${verdict.resolvedModel}, outcome ceiling ${verdict.outcome}${opensPullRequest(verdict.outcome) ? ` (may auto-merge: ${policyExpression(verdict.automerge)})` : ''}, timeout ${verdict.executionTimeout ?? 'none'}s`,
439	    issue: number,
